  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "For what real values of \\(k,\\) other than \\(k=0,\\) does the equation \\(x^2+kx+k^2=0\\) have real roots?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(k<0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(k>0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(k\\ge1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "all values of \\(k\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "no values of \\(k\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1958-p10.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The discriminant is \\[ k^2-4k^2=-3k^2. \\] For every nonzero real \\(k,\\) this is negative, so the quadratic has no real roots.\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Use the discriminant condition for a quadratic to have real roots",
        "Compute \\(k^2-4k^2\\) and use the restriction \\(k\\ne0\\)"
      ],
